  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2ee87e681215b6147a44021cf30db039, type: 3}
  m_Name: 3.Hastings Garden
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  nameOfPoint: Hastings Garden
  coordinatesPoint: {x: 14.508116, y: 35.8978}
  firstPartInfo: "This garden holds a commemoration of the islands\u2019 British
    rule. Lord Rawdon Hastings is buried here, a Governor of Malta - though that
    was eight years after the plague epidemic of 1813.  Yet it is not for Lord Hastings
    that I have come here. From this vantage point, I can look out across Marsamxett
    Harbour to the west, onto Manoel Island. Just as this garden carries Lord Hastings\u2019
    name, the island carries that of another colonial ruler - Antonio Manoel de Vilhena,
    a Grand Master of the Knights of St John. So many places in Malta bear the names
    of the men who laid claim to the island.   De Vilhena built a fort on the island
    - I have a clear view of it from here. During the plague, its isolation made
    it the ideal quarantine hospital. It was from there that Antonio Borg made his
    escape. "
  secondPartInfo: "When the plague came to Malta, Valletta was far more crowded than
    it is now - a third of the island\u2019s hundred thousand residents lived within
    and around its wall. Fertile ground for a disease to fester - and fester it did.
    On the 9th of June 1813, less than two months after the first cases, the government
    newspaper stopped reporting the names of the victims. Listing the names of all
    the dead, we read, would leave no space in the newspaper for anything else. 
    And there was one street that had the worst of it.  I have to cross the city,
    heading for one of the shortest streets - a street whose name suggests dark and
    stagnant depths.  Measures were introduced to limit the spread of the disease. 
    By July, Valletta was divided into six districts that no-one could move between.
    Even within those districts, only one person from each household could leave
    to fetch provisions, and only between the hours of 6 and 10 in the morning. "
  imgMarker: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c98feaebd40d8df449e6d3855ac84352, type: 3}
  imgMain: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f26fa8880b3aeef4b8516c82dc12f2a5, type: 3}
  nameOfStory: Quarantine
  hintOfPOI: "Of course I know the street you mean! You have to go all the way to
    the other side of Valletta, right behind the hospital built by the Knights of
    St John - oh, yes, they weren\u2019t called the Knights Hospitaller for nothing!
    It\u2019s a strange street - most of the city\u2019s streets follow a regular
    grid pattern, but this one doesn\u2019t!"
  solutionOfPOI: Go to Wells Street.
